# Ask the server to stream AxisState for these axes, and stop streaming the rest.
#
# The list is ABSOLUTE, not additive: it is the complete set of axes to watch.
# Sending an empty list stops all polling, which is the resting state -- a rig
# nobody is looking at should have no traffic on its serial ports at all.
#
# Why this is opt-in rather than always on:
#
# A full sweep of one axis is five blocking serial round trips (four sensors and
# a position), and the reply timeouts are 100 ms on the Arduino and 300 ms on the
# CRUX. Watching all five axes continuously is 25 round trips per sweep, on the
# same two ports every motion command has to get through. The v2.0 client watched
# ONE axis, only while it was moving, for exactly that reason -- and this keeps
# that shape while moving the cost to the server, where it is paid once for
# however many clients are looking.
#
# The watch is server-wide, not per client. Two clients watching X do not double
# the polling; they get the same stream. What that means in practice: a client
# should ask for what IT needs and not assume it is the only one, because another
# client may already have asked for more.
string[] axes

# Polls per second per axis. 0 uses the server's configured default.
#
# Be realistic about the ceiling: at five round trips an axis, anything above a
# few Hz per axis is asking the serial link for more than it has, and the
# practical effect is that motion commands start queueing behind sensor reads.
float64 hz
---
bool success

# The axes actually being watched now, which is what was asked for unless a name
# was not recognised.
string[] watching

float64 hz
string message
